Three counterfactual baselines are constructed from neighboring counties on an equal-weighted mean of ZHVI. Baseline counties must have 0 reported NOAA storm events in the pre and post storm timeframe to create an unaffected index.

First Ring (R1) neighboring counties are the group of counties physically connected to the target county. This would represent the closest counterfactual to the storm-affected county, but it absorbs the highest level of spillover effect. R1 represents `r n_events_l1` events across `r n_counties_l1` counties, `r pct_survival_l1`% survival rate.

Second Ring (R2) counties are the adjacent neighbors of the R1 counties, exclusive of any R1 counties. These are isolated from the target county by 1 neighbor, so the physical separation reduces the spillover effect at the cost of greater heterogeneity from the target. R2 represents `r n_events_l2` events across `r n_counties_l2` counties, `r pct_survival_l2`% survival rate.

Regional baseline is constructed from all counties in the local climatologial region as defined by Karl & Koss (1984) that were unaffected by NOAA storm events in the pre and post measurement periods. Regional baseline represents `r n_events_regional` events across `r n_counties_regional` counties, `r pct_survival_regional`% survival rate.

R2 and regional baselines must have at least 2 unaffected counties. The first ring baseline may have 1 or more unaffected counties.

# Event Study Results

## Trajectories

```{r setup-es}
#| output: false
#| results: hide
#| message: false
source(here::here("paper", "R", "event_study.R"))
es <- run_event_study(baselines, here::here("paper", "output", "event_study"))

# Pre-storm significance check (L2, mid tier)
pre_storm_check <- baselines$level2$monthly_dev |>
  filter(tier == "mid", month_t %in% c(-3, -2, -1)) |>
  group_by(month_t) |>
  summarise(
    mean_dev = mean(deviation, na.rm = TRUE),
    se_dev   = sd(deviation, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(n()),
    p_value  = 2 * pt(-abs(mean_dev / se_dev), df = n() - 1),
    .groups  = "drop"
  )

pre_t1_p <- round(pre_storm_check$p_value[pre_storm_check$month_t == -1], 3)
pre_t2_p <- round(pre_storm_check$p_value[pre_storm_check$month_t == -2], 3)
pre_t3_p <- round(pre_storm_check$p_value[pre_storm_check$month_t == -3], 3)
pre_t1_dev <- round(pre_storm_check$mean_dev[pre_storm_check$month_t == -1], 3)
```

```{r fig-es-mid, fig.cap="Post-storm ZHVI trajectory for mid tier housing by baseline method. R1 (first ring) shows the smallest deviation, Regional the largest.", fig.height=3.5}
es$fig_es_mid
```

All three baselines show post-storm appreciation relative to baseline counties across all tiers. The magnitude of measured deviation depends on baseline choice. R1 consistently shows the smallest deviation, consistent with spillover contamination of adjacent counties. R2 and Regional show larger deviations. Regional and R2 baselines diverge significantly from zero by T+4 (95% CI).

Pre-storm deviations at T-1 are marginally significant on R2 mid tier (deviation = `r pre_t1_dev`, p = `r pre_t1_p`), with T-2 borderline (p = `r pre_t2_p`) and T-3 not significant (p = `r pre_t3_p`). The direction is negative as target counties slightly underperform their baseline pre-storm — consistent with mean reversion rather than a parallel trends violation.

